# Static analysis of the shipped code. Tests are analysed separately, at a lower level,
# by phpstan-tests.neon — see the note there.
#
# Level 2 is what src passes cleanly today. The remaining cost of going higher, re-measured
# after the strictness rewrite — the earlier figures here were taken before it and were badly
# out of date (level 5 was recorded as 24):
#
#   level 3 —   4 errors
#   level 4 — 105
#   level 5 — 105
#   level 6 — 186
#   level 7 — 213
#
# Level 3's four are all one cause, and it is the same analyser limitation already excepted
# below: Field\Definition::initialiseDefinition() assigns $optional and $defaultValue, and
# PHPStan models a readonly property as assignable only in the declaring class's own
# constructor. A trait cannot satisfy that. So level 3 costs one more ignore entry and no code
# change, which is a judgement call about whether the level is worth the entry — recorded here
# rather than taken silently.
#
# The jump at level 4 is the real work: it is where unreachable branches and always-true
# conditions start being reported, and the structured types acquired a lot of defensive
# null-checking during the rewrite that the type system now says cannot fire. Several are worth
# fixing on merit rather than for the level. Raising this is tracked in docs/ROADMAP.md.
#
# No baseline file by design: errors get fixed, not recorded. The one exception is below,
# and it is a limitation of the analyser rather than a fault in the code.
parameters:
    level: 2
    paths:
        - src
    treatPhpDocTypesAsCertain: false

    ignoreErrors:
        # A sealed field is configured by AtomicField::with(), which clones with a
        # string-keyed array. That array is opaque to PHPStan, so it sees a field's
        # constructor as the only place a property is ever assigned and narrows the
        # property to whatever it was initialised to — a `?int $maxLength` that starts
        # null is inferred as `null`, and the `!== null` guard in front of it then
        # narrows to *NEVER*.
        #
        # The code is correct: with() really does set these. Only the inference is wrong.
        #
        # Removing this needs PHPStan to model PHP 8.5's clone-with. Note that PHP 8.6's
        # readonly property defaults do NOT fix it on their own — the analyser would still
        # see one initialiser and narrow the same way. The other escape is to assign these
        # properties from typed constructor parameters, which was rejected: a constructor
        # should carry what a field needs to exist, not every constraint it might take.
        #
        # @see docs/ROADMAP.md — "PHPStan: clone-with narrowing"
        # $constraints is declared by Field\Definition and assigned by the *using* class's
        # constructor, as its last statement — the trait cannot assign it, because the properties
        # it is built from are set by that constructor. PHPStan models a readonly property as
        # assignable only in the declaring class's own constructor, so it reports both the
        # declaration and any method reachable from it as unsafe.
        #
        # The code is correct, and the two things that could make it incorrect — a constructor
        # that never assigns it, and a wither that fails to rebuild it — are checked at runtime
        # for every field by Api\SealedFieldTest. That is a stronger guarantee than the analyser
        # was offering, not a weaker one.
        -
            message: '#has an uninitialized readonly property \$constraints\.#'
            path: src/AtomicField.php
        # Same cause, one step further out: defineConstraints() runs during construction and
        # *creates* first-class callables, so PHPStan analyses their bodies in a constructor
        # context. The callables are not invoked until validation, long after
        # initialiseDefinition() has set $optional.
        -
            message: '#Access to an uninitialized readonly property .+::\$optional\.#'
            path: src/Field/Collection.php
        -
            message: '#Comparison operation ".+" between .+ and \*NEVER\* results in an error\.#'
            path: src/Field/*.php
        -
            message: '#^Cannot (call method [a-zA-Z_]+\(\)|access property \$[a-zA-Z_]+) on null\.$#'
            path: src/Field/*.php
